# Database (MongoDB + Mongoose 8)

## Schema conventions

- camelCase field names, `is` prefix on booleans (`isPublished`, `isApproved`, `isActive`).
- `{ timestamps: true }` on every schema; rely on `createdAt`/`updatedAt` rather than adding date fields.
- Denormalize deliberately and comment why, as `Blog.authorName` does.
- Reference fields are named after the model, not suffixed with `Id`.

## The comment reference field is `blog`

`Comment.blog` holds the `Blog` ObjectId. There is no `blogId` field on the document.

```js
await Comment.find({ blog: blogId, isApproved: true })
```

`migrations/20241014000002-add-comment-indexes.js` builds its indexes on `blogId`, so those three indexes match nothing and are not used by any query. Do not copy that field name; if you touch that migration, fix it to `blog`.

## Migrations

- Create with `npm run migrate:create`, never by hand-naming a file.
- Always implement both `up` and `down`. Wrap each in `try/catch`, log through `dbLogger.logMigration`, and re-throw so migrate-mongo records the failure.
- Import the logger as `../src/utils/dbLogger.js`. The three 2024-10-14 migrations use `../utils/dbLogger.js`, which does not resolve — follow `20241129000001-migrate-to-local-storage.js` instead.
- Never edit a migration that has already run somewhere. Add a new one.

## Indexes must be applied in two places

`init-scripts/init-db.sh` runs only when a Docker volume is created fresh, so it covers new machines. Existing databases only get changes through migrations. An index or validator change needs both, or environments drift.

## Users and passwords

Hashing lives in the `pre('save')` hook on `User`, and `User.toJSON` strips `password`. Never hash in a controller, and never persist a password by any path that bypasses `save()` (`updateOne`, `findOneAndUpdate`, and bulk writes all skip the hook).

`.lean()` and `.toObject()` also skip `toJSON`, so a lean user query will leak the password hash — select it away explicitly or drop the field before responding.

## Seeding

`npm run seed` wipes users, blogs, and comments, then reloads from `fixtures/` and copies `seed-images/` into `uploads/blogs/`. It is destructive and development-only. Keep fixture credentials aligned with the root `README.md` (`admin@studysprint.com`).
